{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}, {"id": 3, "type": "video", "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe7n1le02ob08re2vid01", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "", "text": "<strong>Frank: </strong> Hello, Mary. I want to know more about database management systems, and I understand you are an expert on the subject. Were there any data management models during the first half of the 20th century?", "time": "0"}, {"id": 1, "image": "", "text": "<strong>Mary: </strong>  Data management was a big problem during the 1950s. There was no standardized way of storing and accessing data, which means that each program had its own way of managing information.", "time": "19000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "", "text": "<strong>Frank: </strong> Were there any innovations that made the data management process easier?", "time": "34500"}, {"id": 3, "image": "", "text": "<strong>Mary: </strong> Only until the mid-1960s. It's during this decade that programmers create a basic data access system for space missions. After this, other developers decided to create more sophisticated data models.", "time": "39000"}, {"id": 4, "image": "", "text": "<strong>Frank:</strong> How was data managed in these models?", "time": "55500"}, {"id": 5, "image": "", "text": "<strong>Mary:</strong> By the end of the 1960s, most models used a hierarchical system that represented data relationships as a tree. This means that for retrieving information, the program has to read the whole tree from the root node.", "time": "58000"}, {"id": 6, "image": "", "text": "<strong>Frank:</strong> That doesn't sound easy. I know that one of the most influential innovations took place in 1970 after a researcher wrote a paper that talked about the basis for data management. How were the coming generations influenced by this paper?", "time": "74200"}, {"id": 7, "image": "", "text": "<strong>Mary:</strong> This paper proposed a relational database management system. According to this proposal, we store data in tables of related rows and columns.", "time": "91500"}, {"id": 8, "image": "", "text": "<strong>Frank:</strong> Was this model called RDBMS?", "time": "103000"}, {"id": 9, "image": "", "text": "<strong>Mary:</strong> Exactly. Thanks to this model, we now have standardized data structures. Plus, applications are able to take their information from different sources.", "time": "106000"}, {"id": 10, "image": "", "text": "<strong>Frank:</strong> But from what I know, this was a theoretical proposal. Was there someone who put these ideas into practice?", "time": "118000"}, {"id": 11, "image": "", "text": "<strong>Mary:</strong> Short after the paper was published, there were successful attempts for making this model for data management come true.", "time": "126000"}]}, "type": "video"}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "type": "WLC", "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "img_path": "", "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " I can talk about the appearance of database management systems.  ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "  I can list some examples of the first database systems.   ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can discuss facts about the appearance of relational database management systems.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "How do you think people managed data before computers were invented?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Do you think a standardized way of managing data makes the use of technology easier? Why? Why not?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content":